THE CRIME COLD OPEN — MONTAGE STANDARD (DEFAULT) + 5 FALLBACK PATTERNS
================================================================================

▶ TYPE CR-MONTAGE — HOOK-LINE MONTAGE (default — use this unless the case
   genuinely cannot support it, e.g. a very short/simple case)

   This is the required opening format going forward, matching the proven
   viral documentary standard: a rapid, NON-CHRONOLOGICAL burst of 4–7
   standalone shock-lines pulled from LATER in the story (investigation,
   confession, forensic reveal, arrest, twist) — stitched together with
   implied hard cuts (music/visual stings), BEFORE any chronological
   storytelling begins.

   Rules for the montage:
     • 4–7 lines total. Each line is 1–2 sentences, self-contained.
     • Each line must be a shock unit on its own: a name + a shocking act,
       OR a number + a consequence, OR a chilling rhetorical question.
       (e.g. "पुलिस जब लाश को पलटती है तो नीचे एक चिट्ठी मिलती है।" /
       "पत्नी रोते-रोते बयान देती है — लेकिन CDR कुछ और कहती है।" /
       "54 बार टेस्ट के बाद भी FSL एक जवाब नहीं दे पा रही थी — क्यों?")
     • Pull these lines from across the WHOLE case timeline — investigation,
       forensic pivot, confession, arrest, aftermath — deliberately out of
       order. Do not explain any of them. Do not resolve any of them here.
     • Do NOT give away the outcome (who did it / how it ends) — tease
       outcomes, never explain mechanisms.
     • No case name, no victim backstory, no "aaj ki kahani" preamble inside
       the montage itself — pure shock units only.
     • Each montage line should be later PAID OFF verbatim-in-meaning at its
       proper chronological place in the full script — this is the loop-back
       mechanism the viewer's brain tracks subconsciously.

   MANDATORY PIVOT LINE (ends the montage, starts the real narration):
     Immediately after the montage, insert ONE grounding sentence that shifts
     tone from chaotic/rapid to calm/authoritative and opens chronological
     storytelling. Use a "देखिए..." (or case-appropriate equivalent) opener,
     e.g. "देखिए, पूरी कहानी शुरू होती है [date/place] से..."
     This single line replaces the old COLD OPEN PART C strictly for tone —
     it must land BEFORE the case label + stakes line (old Part B) and
     BEFORE victim humanization (Beat 4).

▶ FALLBACK TYPES (use only when a single-scene cold open genuinely serves
   the story better than a montage — rare; state the reason in the validator):

   TYPE CR-A — DISCOVERY SCENE
     A body / object / smell / sound is found. The discovery itself is the hook.

   TYPE CR-B — LAST KNOWN MOMENT
     The victim's last ordinary moment — before everything changed.

   TYPE CR-C — THE IMPOSSIBLE CLAIM
     A statement that sounds too perfect, too rehearsed, too scripted.

   TYPE CR-D — FORENSIC TWIST TEASE
     A science finding revealed upfront — viewer knows result, not the story.

   TYPE CR-E — DIGITAL DECEPTION REVEAL
     A fake identity / social media trap / digital double life as opening hook.

   Fallback types still require the same PIVOT LINE ("देखिए...") immediately
   after them before moving into case label + stakes.

COLD OPEN PART B (after the pivot line): Stakes + victim identity + case
   label + time/place context.
COLD OPEN PART C (after Part B): Story promise —
   "इस वीडियो में हम बताएंगे..." / "इस कहानी की शुरुआत होती है [date] से..."

❌ NEVER OPEN WITH: Press conference / generic preamble /
   "कुछ कहानियाँ रूह तक हिला देती हैं" / killer biography before victim /
   "आज की कहानी बहुत दुखद है"
❌ NEVER let the montage lines resolve themselves or explain their own
   context — that job belongs entirely to the chronological script body.

