PART B — AI IMAGE GENERATION PROMPT (for image prompt tools)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

▸ RULE: Generate a full image prompt for EVERY character in crime stories.
  Crime stories do not involve sitting government officials, so all characters
  get a prompt. Only skip if a character is explicitly a named current
  government officeholder (e.g. sitting CM, sitting PM).

▸ For all characters — victims, perpetrators, police officers, forensic
  experts, family members, witnesses, digital criminals — write a
  complete AI image generation prompt in English.

▸ Every image prompt MUST follow this exact structure and order:

  [PARAGRAPH 1 — PHYSICAL DESCRIPTION]
  Describe as a fictional person. Never use real name.
  Include: age impression, build, height feel, skin tone, face shape,
  eyes (colour/intensity), nose, jawline, brow weight, beard/hair,
  expression, and the overall personality energy they project visually.
  End with their emotional state in the story (e.g. "grief-hollowed",
  "coldly composed", "quietly dangerous", "innocent and unaware").

  [PARAGRAPH 2 — OUTFIT & CULTURAL / OCCUPATIONAL MARKERS]
  Describe the outfit completely. Be precise about:
  — Religion/culture: Muslim taqiyah + jubba; Hindu sindoor + mangalsutra;
    Sikh dastar colour; Christian plain dress; etc.
  — Occupation: police khaki + state + rank + wireless; doctor white coat;
    corporate ID card + formal shirt; forensic scientist lab coat.
  — Victim: what they were wearing in their last known ordinary moment
    (e.g. "simple cotton salwar kameez in muted pink, glass bangles, small
    gold studs — the clothes of a working woman from a modest household").
  — Perpetrator: what they looked like BEFORE arrest — the face of a normal
    person, not a villain. That contrast is the point.
  State that this outfit is fixed and consistent across all images.

  [PARAGRAPH 3 — STYLE DIRECTION]
  Always write exactly: "Style: 2D digital illustration, clean character
  design, detailed face, strong personality focus, realistic proportions,
  [tone word matching character's role] tone, no background story scene,
  no action pose, no extra characters, plain or minimal background,
  character-sheet style, front-facing or slight 3/4 angle."
  Replace [tone word]: victim = sorrowful / perpetrator = composed or cold /
  officer = focused / forensic expert = clinical / family member = grieving.

  [PARAGRAPH 4 — PERSONALITY KEYWORDS]
  5–8 single-word or short-phrase descriptors capturing this character's
  role energy in the story.
  Format: "Personality keywords: [word], [word], [word]..."

  [PARAGRAPH 5 — NEGATIVE PROMPT]
  Customised per character. Always include base negatives:
  no cartoon comedy, no exaggerated features, no crowd scene, no text
  overlay, no logo, no watermark, no real-person likeness.
  Then add character-specific negatives:
  — Victim: no aggressive pose, no villain energy, no dark aesthetic
  — Perpetrator: no obvious villain look, no dramatic evil expression,
    no weapons unless scene requires (the horror is they look normal)
  — Police: no military gear, correct state uniform only
  — Forensic: no crime scene gore, no body parts visible
  — Grieving family: no dramatic performance, no theatrical expression

▸ CRIME-SPECIFIC IMAGE PROMPT RULES:

  VICTIMS: Always portray in their ordinary moment — before the crime.
    The image should feel like a portrait of someone who had a life.
    No distress, no wounds, no crime scene context.
    The viewer should feel the loss just by looking at them.

  PERPETRATORS: Always portray as they appeared to the world — composed,
    ordinary, the face nobody suspected. This is the horror.
    Do NOT make them look like movie villains.
    The contrast between "normal face" and "what they did" is the point.

  POLICE / INVESTIGATORS: Exact state uniform. Professional and focused.
    The "hero" energy comes from their expression, not their pose.

  FORENSIC EXPERTS: Clinical, precise, white-coat authority.
    The science is the weapon — convey that through calm competence.

  FAMILY MEMBERS (as collective): Grief shown through restraint, not drama.
    An empty expression, a held object (photograph, clothes), downcast eyes.

  GROUP CHARACTERS (police team, investigation unit):
    Write one prompt for the unit's visual identity — uniform, bearing,
    collective energy. Note it represents a team, not one individual.

▸ Tone: 2D animation documentary — serious, realistic illustrated people.
  Not manga. Not Disney. Not caricature. Not horror-movie aesthetics.

